 "_note": "The UI MOTION spring corpus \u2014 THE CRUX OF THE 1:1 CLAIM. The authoring plane is SwiftUI's `.spring(response:dampingFraction:)` (that is what `anim=\"spring\"` has always meant on iOS, and animDuration sets the response). The MATH plane is the mass-1 damped oscillator the scene kernel already pins. THE CONVERSION, pinned here: \u03c9\u2099 = 2\u03c0 / response \u00b7 stiffness k = \u03c9\u2099\u00b2 \u00b7 damping coefficient c = 2 \u00b7 dampingFraction \u00b7 \u03c9\u2099 (so \u03b6 = c / (2\u221ak) = dampingFraction exactly). THE VALUE LAW is the analytic mass-1 damped spring from 0 to 1 (initial position 0, initial velocity 0) at REAL elapsed seconds \u2014 sceneSpring, unchanged and unduplicated. THE SETTLE LAW: a spring owns its clock; it completes at T = ln(1000) / (\u03c9\u2099 \u00b7 (\u03b6 \u2212 \u221amax(0, \u03b6\u00b2\u22121))) \u2014 the time its envelope decays to 0.1% \u2014 and progress clamps to EXACTLY 1 at and after T, so ending never snaps. `motionSettleMs(spec)` returns T\u00b71000; for a curve spec it returns the curve duration. Underdamped springs legitimately OVERSHOOT (p > 1) before settling \u2014 the bouncy case pins that. Platform notes: SwiftUI's spring IS this oscillator (response/dampingFraction are its own parameters), and Compose's spring(dampingRatio, stiffness) is the same mass-1 oscillator \u2014 both consume the converted numbers directly. CSS has NO spring, so the web renderer samples this curve into a `linear()` easing (ui-motion.md, the named approximation). All floats 6 decimals, tolerance 1.5e-6; independent scratch derivation.",
